import Mathlib

-- This gets access to most of the manual genre (which is also useful for textbooks)
open Verso.Genre Manual

-- This gets access to Lean code that's in code blocks, elaborated in the same process and
-- environment as Verso
-- Write a code block with ```savedLean ... ``` to save it to an external file.
open Verso.Genre.Manual.InlineLean


open Phrasebook

set_option pp.rawOnError true

#doc (Manual) "Groups" =>

We describe Mathlib's group theory in the sections below.

# Basics

Mathlib contains a theory of groups. Moreover it supports both multiplicative and additive
notation. The following adds a group to the Lean environment using multiplicative notation:
```lean
variable {G : Type*} [Group G]
```

Let `g` and `k` be elements of the group `G`:
```lean
variable {g k : G}
```

The following expresses the associative law:
```lean
example (x y z : G) :
    (x * y) * z = x * (y * z) :=
  mul_assoc x y z
```

We can witness the same mathematics in additive notation as follows:
```lean
variable {A : Type*} [AddGroup A] {a b : A}

example (x y z : A) :
    (x + y) + z = x + (y + z) :=
  add_assoc x y z
```

Commutators of elements are defined with inverses on the right side:
```lean
open scoped commutatorElement in
example : ⁅g, k⁆ = g * k * g⁻¹ * k⁻¹ := rfl

open scoped addCommutatorElement in
example : ⁅a, b⁆ = a + b + -a + -b := rfl
```

For commutative (abelian) groups, use {name}`CommGroup`:
```lean
variable {G' : Type*} [CommGroup G']
```

Morphisms and isomorphisms of groups exist and have a special notation as shown below:
```lean
variable (H : Type*) [Group H]
  (f : G →* H) (e : G ≃* H)
```

# Properties of groups

Use class variables to assert properties of groups:
```lean
-- `G` is nilpotent:
variable [Group.IsNilpotent G]
-- `G` is solvable:
variable [Group.IsSolvable G]
-- `G` is simple:
variable [IsSimpleGroup G]
-- `G` is cyclic:
variable [IsCyclic G]
-- `G` is perfect:
variable [Group.IsPerfect G]
-- `G` is a p-group (π-group): (not a class)
variable {p : ℕ} (h : IsPGroup p G)
-- `G` is a p-group for a prime `p`:
variable {p : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (h : IsPGroup p G)
-- `G` is a Z-group:
variable [IsZGroup G]
-- `G` is finitely generated:
variable [Group.FG G]
-- `G` is finitely presented:
variable [Group.IsFinitelyPresented G]
-- `G` is vritually nilpotent: (not a class)
variable (h : Group.IsVirtuallyNilpotent G)
-- `G` is vritually cyclic:
variable [Group.IsVirtuallyCyclic G]
-- `G` is a torsion group:
variable (h : IsMulTorsion G)
-- `G` is torsion-free:
variable [IsMulTorsionFree G]
-- `G` has unique roots:
variable [HasUniqueRoots G]
-- `G` is rootable:
variable [RootableBy G ℕ]
-- or the more familiar name additive equivalent,
-- `A` is divisible:
variable [DivisibleBy A ℕ]
```

Use {name}`Group.nilpotencyClass` to get the nilpotency class of a group
(or zero if the group is not nilpotent).

Use {name}`Group.rank` to get the rank of a finitely generated group.

For an element `g` of a group `G`, you can query its order and claim it is of finite order:
```lean
-- The order of `g` as a natural number:
-- (or zero if it isn't of finite order)
#check orderOf g
-- The proposition that `g` is of finite order:
#check IsOfFinOrder g
```

Use {name}`Monoid.exponent` to get the exponent of the group.

Since groups are types equipped with additional structure, to get the order of a group use
{name}`Nat.card`, {name}`ENat.card`, or {name}`Cardinal.mk`.
To restrict the size of the group, use classes such as
{name}`Finite`, {name}`Infinite`, or {name}`Nontrivial`:
```lean
-- Let `G` be a finite group
variable {G : Type*} [Group G] [Finite G]
```

# Subgroups

For a group `G`, {name}`Subgroup` `G` is the type of subgroups of `G`.
Let `H` and `K` be subgroups of `G`:
```lean
variable (H K : Subgroup G)
```

Subgroups form a complete lattice, and so we have:
```lean
-- The proposition that a subgroup is contained in another:
#check H ≤ K
-- or strictly contained:
#check H < K

-- The trivial subgroup:
#check (⊥ : Subgroup G)
-- The entire group as a subgroup:
#check (⊤ : Subgroup G)

-- The intersection of two subgroups:
#check H ⊓ K
-- The subgroup generated by the union of two subgroups:
#check H ⊔ K

variable {ι : Sort*} (S : ι → Subgroup G)

-- The intersection of an indexed family of subgroups:
#check ⨅ i, S i
-- The subgroup generated by the union of
-- an indexed family of subgroups:
#check ⨆ i, S i

variable (s : Set (Subgroup G))

-- The intersection of a set of subgroups:
#check sInf s
-- The subgroup generated by the union of
-- a set of subgroups:
#check sSup s
```

Since the pointwise product of two subgroups as sets is not always a subgroup,
it is usually more useful to use `H ⊔ K` whenever you need `H * K`.
If you do need the pointwise product as a set, use
```lean
open scoped Pointwise in
#check (H * K : Set G)
```
and if one of the subgroups is normal you can use {name}`Subgroup.mul_normal`
to relate the pointwise product to `H ⊔ K`.

For an element of the group, use the membership operator to say that a subgroup contains an element:
```lean
variable {g : G}

#check g ∈ H
```

Rarely, you might have to treat a subgroup as a set of elements.
In such cases you can cast a subgroup to a set:
```lean
#check (H : Set G)
```

To get the index of a subgroup use
```lean
-- The index of `H` in `G`:
#check H.index
-- The relative index of `H` as a subgroup of `K`:
#check H.relIndex K
```
Note that when `H` is not contained in `K`, the relative index `H.relIndex K`
considers the index of their intersection `H ⊓ K` inside `K`.

Subgroups can be coerced into a type, so as for groups you can use `Nat.card` to get their order:
```lean
-- Lagrange's theorem:
recall Subgroup.card_subgroup_dvd_card :
  Nat.card H ∣ Nat.card G
recall Subgroup.card_mul_index :
  Nat.card H * H.index = Nat.card G
recall Subgroup.index_eq_card :
  H.index = Nat.card (G ⧸ H)
```

Use class variables to assert properties of subgroups:
```lean
-- `H` is normal in `G`:
variable [H.Normal]
-- `H` is characteristic in `G`:
variable [H.Characteristic]
-- `H` is subnormal in `G`: (not a class)
variable (h : H.IsSubnormal)
-- `H` is of finite index in `G`:
variable [H.FiniteIndex]
-- `H` is of finite index as a subgroup of `K`:
variable [H.IsFiniteRelIndex K]
-- `H` is finitely normally generated: (not a class)
variable (h : H.IsFinitelyNormallyGenerated)
```
Note that when `H` is not contained in `K`, the property `H.IsFiniteRelIndex K`
considers the index of their intersection `H ⊓ K` inside `K`.

When coerced into a type, subgroups have an induced `Group` structure,
so you can also use any property about groups:
```lean
-- `H` is cyclic:
variable [IsCyclic H]
-- `H` is nilpotent:
variable [Group.IsNilpotent H]
```

Some ways to create well-known subgroups:
```lean
variable (s : Set G) (p n : ℕ)

-- The center of the group:
#check Subgroup.center G
-- The centralizer of a set:
#check Subgroup.centralizer s
-- The normalizer of a set:
#check Subgroup.normalizer s
-- The commutator of the group:
#check commutator G
-- The commutator of two subgroups:
#check ⁅H, K⁆
-- The cyclic subgroup generated by an element:
#check Subgroup.zpowers g
-- The subgroup generated by a set:
#check Subgroup.closure s
-- The normal subgroup generated by a set:
#check Subgroup.normalClosure s
-- The normal core of a subgroup:
#check H.normalCore
-- The focal subgroup of a subgroup:
#check H.focalSubgroup
-- The Frattini subgroup of the group:
#check frattini G
-- The torsion subgroup of a commutative group:
#check CommGroup.torsion G'
-- The `p`-primary component of a group:
#check CommGroup.primaryComponent G' p
-- The `n`th element in the derived series of the group:
#check derivedSeries G n
-- The `n`th element in the upper central series
-- of the group:
#check Subgroup.upperCentralSeries G n
-- The `n`th element in the lower central series
-- of a subgroup:
#check H.lowerCentralSeries n
```

For a group homomorphism, the kernel and the range are subgroups:
```lean
variable (f : G →* G')
#check f.ker
#check f.range
```
and given subgroups on either group, the image and preimage are subgroups:
```lean
variable (H : Subgroup G) (H' : Subgroup G')
-- The image of `H` under `f`:
#check H.map f
-- The preimage of `H'` under `f`:
#check H'.comap f
```
A notable special case is the homomorphism {name}`Subgroup.subtype`
which helps to deal with a subgroup of another subgroup:
```lean
variable (H K : Subgroup G)
-- Consider `H` as a subgroup of `K`:
-- (with a type of `Subgroup K`)
#check H.subgroupOf K
-- When `H` is not contained in `K`,
-- this is their intersection `H ⊓ K`.

variable (I : Subgroup H)
-- `I` is a subgroup of `H`,
-- but since `H` is a subgroup of `G`
-- we can consider `I` as a subgroup of `G`:
-- (with a type of `Subgroup G`)
#check I.map H.subtype
```
e.g. you can use {name}`Subgroup.subgroupOf` to say that a subgroup is normal in another one:
```lean
-- `H` is normal as a subgroup of `K`:
variable [(H.subgroupOf K).Normal]
```

# Quotients

For every subgroup `H` of a group `G`, `G ⧸ H` is the type of cosets of `H` in `G`.

Mathlib knows that the cosets of a normal subgroup are naturally a group:
```lean
variable (N : Subgroup G) [N.Normal]
#synth Group (G ⧸ N)
```

The first isomorphism theorem might be illustrative:
```lean
recall QuotientGroup.quotientKerEquivRange
  {G : Type*} [Group G] {H : Type*} [Group H] (φ : G →* H) :
  G ⧸ φ.ker ≃* φ.range
```

# Sylow subgroups

Mathlib bundles Sylow subgroups into a type:
```lean
-- Let `H` be a Sylow `p`-subgroup of `G`
variable (p : ℕ) (H : Sylow p G)
```

Note that like `IsPGroup`, `p` does not have to be prime, although some theorems require it to be.
To require `p` to be prime, use either
```lean
variable (hp : p.Prime)
```
or
```lean
variable [Fact p.Prime]
```

For every group `G` and every natural number `p` there exists at least one Sylow `p`-subgroup,
which you can get noncomputably using either the `Nonempty` instance or the `Inhabited` instance:
```lean
#check Classical.arbitrary (Sylow p G)
#check (default : Sylow p G)
```

Sylow subgroups have a coercion to subgroups and to sets:
```lean
#check (H : Subgroup G)
#check (H : Set G)
```
Note that since subgroups also have a coercion to sets,
you might have to deal with expressions that use two coercions to get to a set:
```lean
#check ((H : Subgroup G) : Set G)
```
You can use the `simp` lemma {name}`Sylow.coe_coe` to normalize it to the direct coercion.

# Notable results

The following are some notable results in Mathlib's group theory library:
- *Lagrange's theorem* {name}`Subgroup.card_subgroup_dvd_card`
- *Sylow's first theorem* {name}`Sylow.exists_subgroup_card_pow_prime`
- *Simplicity of the alternating group* {name}`alternatingGroup.isSimpleGroup`
- The *Schur-Zassenhaus theorem* {name}`Subgroup.exists_left_complement'_of_coprime`
